# Copy this file to .env.acmebot and fill in real values before running:
#   cp .env.acmebot.example .env.acmebot
#   docker compose build
#   docker compose run --rm bootstrap   # one-time: prints an .env block, paste it back in here
#   docker compose run --rm acmebot     # issue/renew + Key Vault import
#
# Note: this is deliberately NOT named .env — the repo root already has an
# unrelated .env (GH_TOKEN for other tooling); reusing that name would either
# clobber it or leak GH_TOKEN into this container, and docker compose
# auto-loads a root .env for its own variable substitution regardless.
#
# See acme-bootstrap.sh, acme-cron-update.sh, and CONTEXT.md for the full
# description of each variable and the DNS persist mode flow.
#
# IMPORTANT: `docker --env-file` does NOT support inline comments — a `#`
# is only treated as a comment when it's the first character of the line.
# `FOO=bar # some note` sets FOO to the literal string "bar # some note",
# not "bar". Put comments/notes on their own line above the variable
# instead, e.g.:
#   # platform-prod
#   AZUREDNS_SUBSCRIPTIONID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

# --- Required (used by both acme-bootstrap.sh and acme-cron-update.sh) ------
# Comma separated list of domains for the certificate's SAN set. Wildcard
# entries (e.g. *.example.com) are supported. acme-cron-update.sh treats the
# first entry as the primary domain (CN); order doesn't matter for bootstrap.
ACME_DOMAINS=example.com,*.example.com
# Email address registered with Let's Encrypt (use a real address).
ACME_EMAIL=you@example.org

# --- Optional (acme-cron-update.sh only) -------------------------------------
# Overrides where acme.sh keeps its config/state and the account key
# (default: /acme.cert.store). Only worth setting if a k8s PVC/volume is
# mounted somewhere other than the container's default — otherwise leave
# unset. Not needed for the plain ACME_ID_SECRET_KEY flow (below), which
# writes account.key here itself on every run regardless.
# ACME_HOME=/acme.cert.store

# Pass acme.sh's own --debug flag through for troubleshooting. 1 -> --debug;
# 2 or 3 -> --debug 2/--debug 3 for more detail (may print secrets — local/CI
# troubleshooting only). Useful to see the raw DNS API request/response, e.g.
# when ACME_DNS_MODE=azure fails with "Invalid domain" (usually means
# AZUREDNS_SUBSCRIPTIONID doesn't match the subscription that actually hosts
# the domain's Azure DNS zone, or the principal lacks list/read rights on it).
# ACME_DEBUG=1

# Key Vault import is opt-in: leave AZ_KV_NAME empty/unset for Phase 1
# local-folder-only mode (certificate is always written to /certs/<primary
# domain> regardless). Set it to also import into Azure Key Vault via
# `az keyvault certificate import`.
# AZ_KV_NAME=my-keyvault
# AZ_KV_CERT_NAME=example-com
# Default (if unset): primary domain with dots replaced by dashes. If set
# explicitly, must be a valid Key Vault object name: 1-127 chars, letters/
# digits/dashes only — no dots, underscores, or wildcards (acme-cron-update.sh
# checks this before attempting the import and fails fast with a clear error).

# Optional: tag the Key Vault certificate on import. Comma and/or
# semicolon (with optional surrounding whitespace) separated k=v pairs —
# passed through to `az keyvault certificate import --tags`. Ignored if
# AZ_KV_NAME is empty/unset. Both separators are accepted interchangeably;
# a tag value containing a literal comma or semicolon cannot be
# represented this way, since it would itself be split as a separator.
# KEYVAULT_CERT_TAGS=env=prod, team=platform

# Optional: use a known PFX password instead of a fresh random one each
# run. Only useful if you need to later export/download the PFX back out
# of Key Vault yourself — the password cannot be changed after import, so
# this only helps for certs imported from here on.
# KEYVAULT_CERT_PFX_PASSWORD=

# --- Optional (Azure auth for local/CI testing; acme-cron-update.sh only) ---
# Only consulted if AZ_KV_NAME above is set. In a real k8s deployment, the
# container's managed identity is used instead (no env vars needed here).
# For local/CI testing without a managed identity, set all three to fall
# back to an explicit `az login --service-principal` — the principal needs
# "Key Vault Certificates Officer" (or equivalent import) rights on
# AZ_KV_NAME. These are NOT auto-detected by the `az` CLI itself (unlike the
# Azure SDKs' DefaultAzureCredential); acme-cron-update.sh passes them to
# `az login` explicitly.
# AZURE_CLIENT_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
# AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# AZURE_TENANT_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

# --- Optional (DNS validation mode; acme-cron-update.sh only) ---------------
# "persist" (default) — DNS persist mode (--dns-persist): no DNS write
# credentials in the CronJob, but not yet validated by production Let's
# Encrypt (see CONTEXT.md/ADR 0002) — only ACME_SERVER=letsencrypt_test
# accepts it today.
# "azure" — fully automated dns-01 via acme.sh's dns_azure plugin against
# the domain's Azure DNS zone API (no `az` CLI/login involved — the plugin
# talks to Azure directly). Works with production Let's Encrypt right now.
# Skips the Bootstrap step entirely (self-registers the ACME account on
# first run). Two auth options:
#   - real k8s deployment: AZUREDNS_MANAGEDIDENTITY=true — uses the pod's
#     managed identity, no client secret needed at all (just needs
#     AZUREDNS_SUBSCRIPTIONID below, and "DNS Zone Contributor" granted to
#     the identity on the domain's Azure DNS zone).
#   - local/CI testing (no managed identity available): reuses the same
#     AZURE_TENANT_ID/AZURE_CLIENT_ID/AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET already set above
#     for the Key Vault import — no separate AZUREDNS_TENANTID/APPID/
#     CLIENTSECRET vars needed (set those explicitly instead only if this
#     domain's DNS zone should use a different, more narrowly-scoped
#     principal than the Key Vault one). Either way, that principal needs
#     "DNS Zone Contributor" on the domain's Azure DNS zone — it's a real
#     secret you create yourself (e.g. via `az ad sp create-for-rbac`);
#     acme.sh does not generate or receive this for you.
# ACME_DNS_MODE=azure
# AZUREDNS_SUBSCRIPTIONID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
# Falls back to AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID if AZUREDNS_SUBSCRIPTIONID is unset —
# only set that instead if you'd rather not repeat it under both names.
# AZUREDNS_MANAGEDIDENTITY=true
# Only needed if this domain's DNS zone should use a different principal
# than AZURE_CLIENT_ID/AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET/AZURE_TENANT_ID above (otherwise
# leave these three unset — they fall back to the Key Vault ones):
# AZUREDNS_TENANTID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
# AZUREDNS_APPID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
# AZUREDNS_CLIENTSECRET=
#
# "aws" — fully automated dns-01 via acme.sh's dns_aws plugin against the
# domain's Route53 hosted zone (no `aws` CLI/login involved — the plugin
# talks to Route53 directly). Works with production Let's Encrypt right
# now. Skips the Bootstrap step entirely (self-registers the ACME account
# on first run). Uses the standard AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID/AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
# names — no ACMEBOT-specific prefix needed. Two auth options:
#   - real ECS/EC2 deployment: leave both unset entirely — the plugin
#     automatically falls back to the container/instance IAM role (ECS
#     task role or EC2 instance profile via IMDS), no secret needed at all.
#   - local/CI testing (no IAM role available): set both below — a real
#     access key you create yourself (e.g. via `aws iam create-access-key`);
#     acme.sh does not generate or receive this for you.
# Either way, that role/user needs Route53 ChangeResourceRecordSets/
# GetChange/ListHostedZones* rights on the domain's hosted zone.
# ACME_DNS_MODE=aws
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# --- Optional -----------------------------------------------------------------
# ACME server to target. Defaults to letsencrypt_test (staging) so debugging
# can't hit production rate limits; set letsencrypt deliberately for real,
# trusted certificates (see CONTEXT.md).
# ACME_SERVER=letsencrypt

# Domain certificate key type/size. Defaults to 2048 (RSA-2048) — acme.sh
# itself defaults to EC-256 if not told otherwise, but Azure Key Vault's PFX
# certificate import has real, documented problems with EC-keyed certs, so
# RSA is forced here. Override with e.g. "4096", or "ec-256"/"ec-384" only
# if EC import is confirmed reliable for your vault.
# ACME_KEYLENGTH=2048

# Optional guard rail: fail fast (before the Key Vault import) if the issued
# certificate's chain doesn't link back to this root CA. Unset/empty by
# default (opt-in, no check). Set to a substring of the expected root's
# issuer, e.g. Let's Encrypt production's trust anchor:
# SAFETY_ALERT_IF_ROOT_CA_NOT=CN=ISRG Root X1
# Leave unset while testing against ACME_SERVER=letsencrypt_test (staging) —
# its chain never contains "ISRG Root X1", so this check would always fail.

# Renewal check: before ever calling acme.sh, if AZ_KV_NAME is set,
# acme-cron-update.sh queries Key Vault for the current certificate's
# expiry and skips issuance entirely if it's still valid more than
# RENEWAL_THRESHOLD_DAYS away — safe to run on every cron tick without
# needlessly re-issuing (and burning Let's Encrypt's rate limit) each time,
# even though acme.sh's own internal skip-if-not-due state (under
# ACME_HOME) does NOT persist across container runs here.
# This Key Vault check and the ACME_DNS_MODE=azure DNS zone access check
# (above) ALWAYS both run every time, regardless of what the other one
# finds, and any failures from either are reported together in one
# combined error. Once both pass, a one-line summary is printed at the end
# (new cert added / renewed / force-renewed / update skipped + days left).
# The Key Vault check also compares the existing certificate's SANs against
# ACME_DOMAINS (best-effort) — a mismatch always forces re-issuance
# regardless of expiry, since AZ_KV_CERT_NAME is just a fixed label and
# nothing stops the domain list changing later while it stays the same.
# RENEWAL_THRESHOLD_DAYS=30
# Force issuance/renewal even if Key Vault's existing cert is still valid
# well beyond RENEWAL_THRESHOLD_DAYS (e.g. domains changed, or testing).
# FORCE_RENEW=true

# Migration safety guard rail: set to "true" to REFUSE to issue/import
# unless the existing Key Vault certificate under AZ_KV_CERT_NAME is found
# AND its SANs are confirmed to already match ACME_DOMAINS. Unset/default
# "false" only warns on a mismatch (or unverifiable SAN list) and still
# proceeds. Use this once, temporarily, when cutting an existing
# already-in-production certificate (issued by some other CA/process) over
# to this script — it protects against a typo'd AZ_KV_NAME/AZ_KV_CERT_NAME
# or wrong ACME_DOMAINS silently creating a new/wrong certificate or
# overwriting an unrelated one. Applies even with FORCE_RENEW=true.
# SAFETY_ONLY_UPD_IF_EXISTING_SAN_MATCH=true

# Key Vault refuses any new certificate create/import for AZ_KV_CERT_NAME
# while that name has a "pending" certificate operation stuck in status
# "inProgress" (e.g. a CSR-based "Generate" started via the Portal/CLI and
# never merged/canceled, or a prior crashed run of this script) — checked
# as part of the Key Vault pre-check above, on every run, before
# FORCE_RENEW/SAN checks, and is a hard pre-flight failure by default (with
# the exact `az keyvault certificate pending delete` command to fix it
# printed in the error). Set to "true" to instead have this script cancel
# a stale "inProgress" pending operation itself and proceed — only do this
# once you've confirmed nothing else is legitimately mid-flight against
# this certificate name.
# KEYVAULT_CANCEL_PENDING_CERT_OP=true

# --- Printed by acme-bootstrap.sh (required by acme-cron-update.sh) ----------
# No volume backs ACME_HOME for either the `docker run` or `docker compose`
# flows in this repo, so acme-bootstrap.sh's printed .env block IS the only
# place the Account key lives between the two steps — paste its full output
# here in place of the placeholders below (it repeats ACME_DOMAINS/
# ACME_EMAIL/ACME_SERVER too, which is fine to overwrite with the same
# values). ACME_ID_SECRET_KEY is required for acme-cron-update.sh to run.
# ACME_DNS_KEY/_VALUE are informational only here (the record must
# already be published by hand); acme-cron-update.sh doesn't read them.
#
# `docker run --env-file`/compose's `env_file:` use a plain KEY=VALUE
# parser: no quoting and no multiline values (a bare continuation line
# breaks it — see README.md). So ACME_ID_SECRET_KEY must stay a single
# line, with the PEM's real newlines written as literal `\n`, exactly as
# acme-bootstrap.sh prints it — do not reformat it or add quotes.
# ACME_ID_SECRET_KEY=-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\n...\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----
# ACME_DNS_KEY=_validation-persist.example.com
# ACME_DNS_VALUE=letsencrypt.org; accounturi=...; policy=wildcard
#
# acme-bootstrap.sh also prints empty AZ_KV_NAME=/AZ_KV_CERT_NAME= placeholders
# in the same block purely as a reminder to fill them in here (or leave them
# empty for Phase 1 local-folder-only mode) — see the "Optional
# (acme-cron-update.sh only)" section above; acme-bootstrap.sh itself never
# reads or needs them.
